{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":73995272,"ns":0,"title":"Meizodon krameri","revisions":[{"revid":1354690436,"parentid":1349490720,"minor":true,"timestamp":"2026-05-17T20:30:13Z","size":3433,"sha1":"b23c66455fce2cf984326678bacdb571fb565404","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Species of snake}}\n{{Speciesbox\n| status = DD\n| status_system = IUCN3.1\n| status_ref = <ref name=iucn>{{cite iucn |author=Spawls, S. |author-link=species:Stephen Spawls |author2=Branch, W.R. |author2-link=William Roy Branch |author3=Malonza, P. |author3-link=species:Patrick Kenyatta Malonza |year=2022 |amends=2014 |title=''Meizodon krameri '' |volume=2022 |article-number=e.T13265429A217783846 |doi=10.2305/IUCN.UK.2022-1.RLTS.T13265429A217783846.en |access-date=9 June 2023}}</ref>\n| genus = Meizodon\n| species = krameri\n| authority = [[Beat Schätti|Schätti]], 1985\n| synonyms = \n}}\n\n'''''Meizodon krameri''''', also known [[Common name|commonly]] as the '''Tana Delta smooth snake''', is a [[species]] of snake in the [[subfamily]] [[Colubrinae]] of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Colubridae]]. The species is [[Endemism|endemic]] to [[Kenya]].<ref name=RDB>{{NRDB species|genus=Meizodon|species=krameri}}</ref>\n\n==Etymology==\nThe [[Specific name (zoology)|specific name]], ''krameri'', is in honor of Swiss herpetologist [[species:Eugen Kramer|Eugen Kramer]].<ref>[[species:Bo Beolens|Beolens B]], [[species:Michael Watkins|Watkins M]], [[species:Michael Grayson|Grayson M]] (2011). ''The Eponym Dictionary of Reptiles''. Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University Press. xiii + 296 pp. {{ISBN|978-1-4214-0135-5}}. (''Meizodon krameri'', p. 146).</ref>\n\n==Description==\n''M. krameri'' may attain a total length (including tail) of {{cvt|50|cm|in}}. The pupil of the eye is round. The [[dorsal scales]] are smooth and are arranged in 19 rows at midbody. Each dorsal scale has one apical pit. The [[Ventral scales|ventrals]] number 166–176, which is fewer than other species in its [[genus]]. The [[anal scale|anal plate]] is divided. The [[Subcaudal scales|subcaudals]] number 68–69 and are also divided. The maxillary teeth number 26, which is more than other species in the genus. [[Dorsum (anatomy)|Dorsally]], ''M. krameri'' is uniformly olive-brown.  The [[Labial scales|labials]] are lighter, as are the centers of the ventrals.<ref name=Spawlsetal2018>Spawls S, [[species:Kim Monroe Howell|Howell K]], [[species:Harald Hinkel|Hinkel H]], [[species:Michele Menegon|Menegon M]] (2018). ''A Field Guide to East African Reptiles, Second Edition''. London, Oxford, New York, New Delhi, Sydney: Bloomsbury Wildlife. 624 pp. {{ISBN|978-1-399-40481-5}}. (''Meizodon krameri'', pp. 480–481).</ref>\n\n==Natural history==\nNothing is known about the [[natural history]] of ''M. krameri'' because it was described in 1985 from two preserved specimens which had been collected in 1934. No additional specimens have been collected or photographed. It may be [[Terrestrial animal|terrestrial]], [[Diurnality|diurnal]], and [[Oviparity|oviparous]] like its [[Congener (biology)|congeners]].<ref name=Spawlsetal2018/>\n\n==References==\n{{Reflist}}\n\n==Further reading==\n*[[species:Beat Schätti|Schätti B]] (1985). \"Systematics of East African Species of ''Meizodon'' [[Johann Gustav Fischer|Fischer]], 1856 (Reptilia Serpentes Colubridae)\". ''Monitore Zoologico Italiano, Supplemento'' '''20''' (8): 149–175. (''Meizodon krameri'', new species, p. 155).\n\n==External links==\n* {{Wikispecies-inline}}\n\n\n{{Taxonbar|from=Q3304772}}\n\n[[Category:Meizodon]]\n[[Category:Reptiles described in 1985]]\n[[Category:Reptiles of Kenya]]\n[[Category:Endemic fauna of Kenya]]\n[[Category:Taxa named by Beat Schätti]]\n\n\n{{snake-stub}}"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T06:31:22Z","lastrevid":1354690436,"length":3433}]}}